er that  to debate and implement an agreement if  one is come to  so that's the key period when parliament  needs to be sitting and parliament will  be sitting  but it also crucially means we can have  a new queen's speech and we can get back  to talking about the nhs dealing with  crime improving  but that will lead to time won't it  because after the queen's speech  there'll be four or five days of debate  then that also eats up time  and you have to admit mps if they'd have  been allowed to continue  without this suspension of parliament  they would have voted to stay put  instead of disappear for party  conference season well  let me answer your two points  individually um the debate on the  queen's speech  has nominal um areas to debate day by  day  but actually you can speak on any matter  relating to the queen's speech so people  could spend the whole of those four to  five days  debating uh leaving the european union  and they could put down an amendment  to the queen's speech or to the humble  address uh  thanking the queen for her speech um  saying  things about the european union so that  is four or five days which can be  debated to brexit  in terms of the conference recess at my  first  and so far only outing at the dispatch  box i referred to the conference recess  and said i expected it would go ahead  and the opposition chief whip nodded in  agreement and that's all recorded in  hansard  so it had been agreed that the  conference recess would go ahead  as it always does parliament hasn't sat  in late september for 80 years  do you admit though that this has made  it harder for mps to stop  a no deal you won't admit it's the main  aim of this move but it may be a  convenient byproduct  i don't think it is actually i think  those who don't want to leave the  european union  um have to um screw their carriage to  the sticking point  that uh there are two ways for them to  stop us leaving the european union  one is to change the government and the  other